const body = `<h1>split() &amp; Data Lines</h1>

<p>
    <code>split</code> is the bridge between text and data. One line of text in, an
    array of pieces out. It was added to the exam's Java Quick Reference in the
    2025 revision, and that is not a small fact: it means every exam from 2026 on
    can hand you a line of data and expect you to take it apart. Loaders, parsers,
    save files, datasets: all of it runs on this one method.
</p>

<h2>The Signature</h2>

<pre><code>String[] split(String del)</code></pre>

<p>Called on a String, with a delimiter. Returns an ARRAY of Strings. The
delimiter itself is removed. The pieces are in order.</p>

<pre><code>String loot = "sword,iron,50";
String[] parts = loot.split(",");

parts[0]        // "sword"
parts[1]        // "iron"
parts[2]        // "50"       (a STRING, not the number 50)
parts.length    // 3</code></pre>

<h2>The Four Rules</h2>

<ol>
    <li><strong>Pieces are always Strings.</strong> Convert with
    <code>Integer.parseInt</code> or <code>Double.parseDouble</code> (JQR, Integer
    class table) when you need numbers.</li>
    <li><strong>Empty fields survive as empty Strings.</strong>
    <code>"dragon,,500".split(",")</code> has length 3, and <code>parts[1]</code>
    is <code>""</code>. It is not null, and it is not skipped.</li>
    <li><strong>split does not trim spaces.</strong> <code>"a, b".split(",")</code>
    gives <code>["a", " b"]</code>. That leading space will crash
    <code>parseInt(" b")</code>. Clean it (trim by hand or substring) or design
    your files without spaces after delimiters.</li>
    <li><strong>Trailing empty pieces can vanish.</strong>
    <code>"a,b,".split(",")</code> has length 2, not 3: Java drops trailing empty
    strings. Leading and middle empties stay. This asymmetry is weird, true, and
    occasionally testable.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Choosing a Delimiter</h2>

<table>
    <tr><th>Delimiter</th><th>Good for</th><th>Danger</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>","</code></td><td>CSV files, item databases</td><td>data containing commas (names like "Blade, the Cruel")</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>" "</code></td><td>commands: "attack goblin"</td><td>multiple spaces create empty pieces; data with spaces breaks entirely</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>";"</code></td><td>save games with text fields</td><td>rare in data, so collisions are rare</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>"|"</code></td><td>save games, pipe-separated</td><td>almost never appears in real text. The professional's choice.</td></tr>
</table>

<p>The design rule: your delimiter must never appear inside your data. That is
why the course data files use commas for numbers and single-word names, and why
7.6b asks what character could NEVER appear in a player name.</p>

<h2>The Standard Loader Pattern</h2>

<pre><code>// one record per line, one field per piece
String line = "goblin 8 3 25 slime";
String[] f = line.split(" ");

String name = f[0];
int hp = Integer.parseInt(f[1]);
int atk = Integer.parseInt(f[2]);
int gold = Integer.parseInt(f[3]);</code></pre>

<p>With a guard, which every real loader has:</p>

<pre><code>String[] f = line.split(" ");
if (f.length == 5) {
    // safe to use f[0] .. f[4]
} else {
    System.out.println("bad line: " + line);
}</code></pre>

<h2>Tinker Challenge: Parser Rewrite</h2>

<div class="tinker">
    <h3>Your 2.2c Parser, Shrunk</h3>
    <p>Your indexOf/substring command parser from 2.2c was probably 10-15 lines.
    Rewrite it around split:</p>
    <pre><code>String command = "attack goblin";
String[] words = command.split(" ");
String verb = words[0];
String target = words.length &gt; 1 ? words[1] : "none";</code></pre>
    <p>Then stress it with the 2.2c edge cases: "attack    dragon" (many spaces),
    leading and trailing spaces, "attack" alone, empty string. Old split-on-space
    truth: multiple spaces make empty pieces. Print <code>words.length</code> for
    each case and watch the count lie to you. Fix it by collapsing spaces or
    rejecting lines that start or end with the delimiter.</p>
</div>

<h2>Bug Hunt</h2>

<div class="bug-hunt">
    <h3>The Three Loader Bugs</h3>
    <pre><code>// Bug 1: numbers that never became numbers
int total = 0;
total += parts[2];              // parts[2] is "50", a String

// Bug 2: the unguarded index
int hp = Integer.parseInt(f[1]);   // f.length might be 1

// Bug 3: the space after the comma
int v = Integer.parseInt(f[2]);    // f[2] is " 50"</code></pre>
    <p>For each: what is the exact failure (compile error, exception, or silent
    wrong answer)? Silent wrong answers are the worst kind; which of these can
    produce one? (Only none: all three are loud. The silent killer is rule 4, the
    vanished trailing piece, when you compute an average over parts.length.)</p>
</div>

<h2>Socratic Checkpoint</h2>

<div class="socratic">
    <h3>Why was split added to the JQR in 2025?</h3>
    <p>The revised CED added file reading (4.6) and datasets (4.2). Both live on
    text lines that must become data. Without split, file questions require
    tedious indexOf/substring work that tests finger skills, not thinking. With
    split, the exam can test the real skill: parsing, guarding, and aggregating.
    The sheet follows the curriculum.</p>
    <h3>Why does split return an array and not an ArrayList?</h3>
    <p>It is a String method, and String lives in java.lang, which cannot know
    about java.util.ArrayList (that would be a backwards dependency). Arrays are
    the lowest common denominator. You meet arrays formally in Unit 6 and convert
    freely afterward.</p>
</div>

<h2>See Also</h2>
<ul>
    <li><a href="string.html">The String Class</a> — the family split belongs to</li>
    <li><a href="../unit-07/reading-files.html">Reading Files (Unit 7.6)</a> — where split carries whole programs</li>
    <li><a href="../unit-06/datasets.html">Working with Data Sets (Unit 6.6)</a> — split on real data</li>
    <li><a href="../../exam/java-quick-reference.html">Java Quick Reference</a> — the split row, verbatim</li>
</ul>`;
